From 70077c75176f9c56874111749566f0d3f9d69579 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EcoWoods Build <build@ecowoods.ca>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 18:47:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(convert): the page that earns the click can now take the
 job
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two independent audits crawled this site and opened with the same sentence. It
had survived thirty-nine guards, because every guard here asks whether a
manifest agrees with itself, and none asked whether the site does what it says.

  <form> count 0. <input> count 0. On every page.

F-160 — THE ASK, ON THE PAGE THAT EARNED IT

The estimate form existed only inside a React modal, behind
`{estimateModalOpen && …}`, on the homepage. Every commercial page's primary
button pointed at `/#quote` — a different url, whose form appears only after
hydration and a click. A business publishing 286 pages of sourced authority was
collecting jobs through a phone number.

`<EstimateForm>` is a real `<form method="post" action="/api/leads">`, rendered
in HTML, mounted above every explanatory section on all five commercial pages.
With scripting off, a broken bundle or a hydration error, the browser posts it
and the route answers with a 303. With JS it submits inline and never leaves the
page. `/api/leads` now reads form-encoded bodies through the SAME validation and
the SAME capture order — the durable log still runs before the rate limiter, and
verify-conversion.mjs now asserts that ordering, because a second parsing path
that skipped it would lose a lead exactly when the client is least capable.

F-161 — A FILTER THAT COULD NEVER RUN

/api/knowledge was `export const dynamic = 'force-static'`. Next prerenders such
a route once at build time and `searchParams` is then ALWAYS EMPTY, so `?q=` and
`?collection=` — both advertised in the route's own `meta.usage` — silently
returned the entire 330 KB corpus. Both audits measured the symptom: three
different queries, byte-identical responses, identical hash. Neither could see
the cause, because nothing in the response says the query was discarded.

Now dynamic. The edge still caches; its key is the full URL including the query.
`meta.filtered` and `meta.returned` say what happened, so the ambiguity that hid
this for months cannot re-form. The generalised check is the most valuable thing
in the new guard: force-static plus searchParams is not an error, not a warning,
and invisible in the response.

F-162 — A DOWNLOAD WITH NOTHING BEHIND IT

/papers said every paper was "downloadable as a PDF" and the API emitted a
versioned `pdfUrl` for all five, while apps/web/public/papers/ was empty. Every
one 404'd. The sentence is retired and `pdfUrl` is now gated on
`pdfIsPublished()` — the same filesystem check the page uses. An agent that
follows a machine surface to a 404 stops trusting the surface.

F-165 — 99.7%, AND THE FENCE THAT WAS NEVER ENFORCED

"99.7% dust capture" was live on the homepage, the dust-free service page, all
32 service-area pages, two guides, FloorForge and /llms.txt — and inside FAQ
answers that become FAQPage JSON-LD, which is the `schema` context the claim
registry had explicitly fenced it out of. The fence was written and never
enforced.

It is almost certainly a HEPA filter's certified rating quoted as a room-capture
fraction. Those are different measurements, and the first general contractor
with a particle counter ends the argument — taking the credibility of every
sourced figure standing beside it. Removed everywhere, replaced with what is
true: HEPA-sealed extraction at the machine, containment at the room, and no
published percentage because none has been measured on a Toronto job.

F-166 — THE GUARD WAS READING THE CODE AND SKIPPING THE COPY

verify-business-facts.mjs scanned app, lib, public and packages. It did not scan
apps/web/content — where every article and case study lives, all of it published,
mirrored to .md and fed to the knowledge API. Adding it surfaced eighteen
retired claims, including four case studies each publishing
"Customer Satisfaction | 4.9/5" with no instrument behind it, on the very pages
whose job is to be evidence. The particle counts stay; they are real readings.
The derived capture percentages and the invented ratings do not.

ALSO

  · F-163 the header now names the five commercial pages. It used to spend five
    of nine slots on homepage anchors (#gallery, #process, #craft, #reviews,
    #faq) that point at nothing on the other 280 pages, plus a product name and
    a login. 69 characters out, 61 in — narrower, and .topbar-nav clips
    silently, so width was the constraint. Nothing was deleted; FloorForge kept
    a real footer link, which verify-links.mjs demanded within one run.
  · F-164 "Now booking · Spring 2026" read that way on 28 August 2026. Derived
    from the clock now. A stale season on the one element whose job is to say
    the business is paying attention says the opposite.
  · Copper text moved to --copper-text: 2.97:1 → 4.81:1. --copper stays the
    fill colour.
  · verify-link-density.mjs now counts a rendered <EstimateForm> as a CTA. It
    stopped seeing one on the commercial pages the day they got a real one.

NOT IN THIS PATCH, AND NOT BECAUSE THEY DO NOT MATTER

The two audits' other P1s are the old domain still serving 200 and the absent
Google Business Profile. Both were confirmed. Neither is code — one is DNS and
host access, the other is a form and a postcard. The redirect map has been
written and checked in old-domain/ for a week.

40 guards green.

